Sandbox hardening from the plugin API security review.

Unix sockets: a grant names a canonical path, and a connection is allowed only
when the requested path still canonicalizes to that exact grant. net.connect
and fetch then connect to the canonical path. A symlink planted under a granted
name later resolves elsewhere and is refused, so it cannot lead to another
socket such as the host's IPC socket.

fetch never uses a proxy. A proxy resolves names on its own side, which would
skip the public-address filter and reach the local network. The test-only
client that existed just to turn proxies off is gone as well.

Public addresses: IPv4-compatible, NAT64 (64:ff9b::/96) and 6to4 (2002::/16)
addresses are judged by the IPv4 address inside them. Local-use NAT64,
Teredo, documentation, discard-only and site-local IPv6 ranges, plus the IPv4
special-purpose, benchmarking and documentation ranges, count as private.

DNS: each lookup runs on its own thread, with at most 64 in flight. Before,
two shared workers ran every lookup, so two names that never answer stalled
all of them. A lookup that times out keeps its slot until getaddrinfo returns,
so hung names cannot pile up threads.

TLS: the `insecure` option is removed. It accepted any certificate, so the
connection was encrypted but not authenticated. A self-signed server is
trusted by pinning its certificate.

Frozen builtins: the freeze now also covers the constructors and prototypes of
Boolean, Symbol, BigInt, RegExp, Date, Promise, Map, Set, WeakMap, WeakSet,
ArrayBuffer, the typed arrays, every Error type, and the iterator, generator
and async function prototypes. The name and message properties on the Error
prototypes become accessors, so `this.name = ...` in an error subclass still
works on a frozen prototype.

